Miguel uses 2 pads of paper per week, each containing 30 sheets. 

First, calculate the sheets used per week:
\[
2 \text{ pads/week} \times 30 \text{ sheets/pad} = 60 \text{ sheets/week}
\]

Next, assume a month has 4 weeks:
\[
60 \text{ sheets/week} \times 4 \text{ weeks/month} = 240 \text{ sheets/month}
\]

\boxed{240}

John picks 4 bananas on Wednesday, 6 on Thursday, and triple the Wednesday amount (4 × 3 = 12) on Friday. Adding these together:  
4 (Wednesday) + 6 (Thursday) + 12 (Friday) = 22.

\boxed{22}

To determine how many days Alfie needs to fly half the Earth's circumference, first calculate half of 40,000 km, which is 20,000 km. Then divide this distance by his daily flight of 400 km:

\[
\frac{20,000 \text{ km}}{400 \text{ km/day}} = 50 \text{ days}
\]

\boxed{50}

